oldValue": "John of Würzburg was a German priest who made a pilgrimage to the Holy Land in the 1160s and wrote a book describing the Christian holy places, the Descriptio terrae sanctae.", "newValue": "Johannes von Würzburg, a German priest likely active in the mid-12th century, became a pivotal figure in the medieval understanding of the Holy Land through his detailed account, the *Descriptio terrae sanctae*. Undertaking a pilgrimage to the Levant around 1165, he meticulously documented the Christian holy places, providing one of the most comprehensive and influential topographical guides for subsequent pilgrims and scholars during an era marked by intense European engagement with the Crusader States.

His *Descriptio*, written in Latin, offers a vivid, firsthand perspective on Jerusalem, Bethlehem, Nazareth, and numerous other biblical sites, often detailing their architectural features, local traditions, and associated relics. Johannes's work stands out for its methodical approach, describing routes, distances, and the specific locations of shrines, churches, and monasteries, thereby serving as both a travelogue and a practical handbook. His narrative provides invaluable insights into the daily life and political landscape of the Latin Kingdom of Jerusalem just decades before its catastrophic defeat at Hattin in 1187, capturing a unique snapshot of crusader society and its sacred geography.

